issues.problematic_code_snippet:
smtp = (smtplib.SMTP_SSL if (self.use_tls and not self.opportunistic_tls) else smtplib.SMTP)(
    self.server, self.port, timeout=10, local_hostname=local_host or None
)

findings.body:
Before this PR, the opportunistic-TLS path explicitly sent `EHLO <self.server>` both before and after `starttls()`. It now sends `local_host`:

::diff{file="api/libs/smtp.py" lines="25-36"}

When `SMTP_LOCAL_HOSTNAME` is unset, which is the default for every existing deployment, `local_host` is `""`. `smtplib.SMTP.ehlo(name='')` falls back to `self.local_hostname`, which is `socket.getfqdn()`. In Docker that's usually a random container ID like `3f9a1c2b7d4e`.

::callout{type="warning"}
This is a silent behavior change for STARTTLS users who never touch the new variable. Relays that check the HELO name (or that worked only because of the old `self.server` workaround) may start rejecting mail. That's the exact failure this feature is meant to prevent.

This PR should be opt-in. Keep the old EHLO name unless the operator actually sets an override:

::suggestion{file="api/libs/smtp.py" lines="33-36"}
            if self.use_tls and self.opportunistic_tls:
                ehlo_name = local_host or self.server
                smtp.ehlo(ehlo_name)
                smtp.starttls()
                smtp.ehlo(ehlo_name)

If you also want the initial connect to stay exactly as before, only pass `local_hostname` when it's set.
